/* CSO Cyber Range — Course content: AC-307 Reverse Engineering & Advanced Malware Analysis
   (Defensive analysis of malicious samples in an authorized, isolated lab — for detection,
   response, and threat intelligence. No development of offensive malware.) */
window.COURSE_DATA=window.COURSE_DATA||{};
window.COURSE_DATA["AC-307"]={
ov:"Analyze malicious software the way a defender must: safely, systematically, and to produce actionable intelligence. This course builds the skills to triage, disassemble, detonate, defeat obfuscation, and dissect real-world and nation-state toolkits — always in an authorized, isolated lab and always for defensive ends: detection engineering, incident response, and threat intelligence. You will progress from static file triage through assembly and disassembly, dynamic sandboxing and behavioral analysis, unpacking and anti-analysis defeat, configuration and IOC extraction, C2 analysis, and finally reporting and attribution — culminating in reverse engineering a sample to extract its configuration blocks, target IPs, and communication mechanisms.",
mods:[
{t:"Malware Analysis Fundamentals and Lab Safety",d:"Why we analyze malware, and how to do it without getting burned.",
obj:["Explain the goals of defensive malware analysis","Describe a safe, isolated analysis lab","Apply handling discipline to live malicious samples"],
read:["Malware analysis is the disciplined study of malicious software to answer defensive questions: what does this sample do, how do we detect it, how do we contain and remediate it, and who might be behind it. It exists to serve incident response (understanding an active intrusion), detection engineering (building signatures and behavioral rules), and threat intelligence (attributing and tracking adversaries). Analysis is not an end in itself — every technique in this course produces something a defender uses: an indicator of compromise, a detection rule, a remediation step, or an intelligence assessment. The discipline distinguishes two complementary approaches: static analysis (examining the sample without running it) and dynamic analysis (observing it as it executes in a controlled environment), and mature analysis interleaves both, because each reveals what the other hides.",
"Handling live malware safely is the non-negotiable foundation, because a real sample is a loaded weapon that will try to spread, phone home, or destroy data the instant it runs. The analysis lab must be isolated: virtual machines with snapshots so a detonated sample can be rolled back, no bridge to production networks, controlled or simulated internet (a fake-network service like INetSim, or a carefully monitored gateway) so the sample's callbacks are observed without giving a real adversary a live victim, and strict discipline about never running samples on a host connected to anything you care about. Samples are handled defanged where possible (renamed, stored in password-protected archives so they are not accidentally executed or scanned into quarantine), and the analyst always assumes the sample is smarter and more hostile than it looks. This safety posture — isolation, snapshots, controlled network, and handling discipline — is what makes the rest of the course possible without causing the very incident you are trying to understand."],
ex:"An incident responder receives a suspicious executable pulled from a compromised workstation. Before touching it, she copies it into an isolated analysis VM with a clean snapshot, disconnects the VM from any real network and points it at a simulated-internet service, and stores the original in a password-protected archive. Only then does she begin triage — the isolation and snapshot mean that whatever the sample does, she can observe it fully and roll back cleanly.",
refs:[["Practical Malware Analysis (Sikorski & Honig), lab setup","Building a safe analysis environment."],["SANS FOR610 lab guidance","Isolation, snapshots, and simulated internet."]],
kc:[
{q:"The purpose of defensive malware analysis is to:",o:["Build better malware","Produce actionable defensive output — IOCs, detection rules, remediation steps, and intelligence","Show off","Nothing practical"],a:1,x:"Every analysis technique serves detection, response, or intelligence — the defensive outputs that protect the organization."},
{q:"A safe analysis lab requires:",o:["Running samples on your work laptop","Isolated VMs with snapshots, no bridge to production, and controlled or simulated internet","A live production network","No precautions"],a:1,x:"Isolation, snapshots, and controlled/simulated network let you observe malware fully without causing a real incident."},
{q:"Static and dynamic analysis relate how?",o:["They are the same","They are complementary — each reveals what the other hides, so mature analysis interleaves both","Only static matters","Only dynamic matters"],a:1,x:"Static (not running) and dynamic (running in a controlled lab) analysis complement each other; good analysis uses both together."}]},
{t:"Static Analysis and File Triage",d:"Learning a sample's nature without running it.",
obj:["Perform initial triage with hashes, strings, and file typing","Interpret PE structure and imports","Recognize signs of packing and capability from static data"],
read:["Static analysis begins with triage: quick, safe techniques that characterize a sample before any execution. The first steps are identifying the file (its true type, not just its extension — a .jpg that is actually a PE executable is an immediate red flag), computing cryptographic hashes (MD5/SHA-256) to fingerprint it and check against threat-intelligence databases, and extracting printable strings, which frequently reveal URLs, IP addresses, file paths, registry keys, command strings, error messages, and even attacker artifacts left in the binary. Strings alone often answer the first questions — a sample containing a hardcoded C2 domain and a ransom-note template tells you a great deal before you run anything. Static triage is fast, safe, and the foundation on which deeper analysis builds.",
"For Windows malware, understanding the Portable Executable (PE) format unlocks the next layer. The PE headers describe how the file loads; the section table reveals its layout (and anomalies — a tiny raw section that expands hugely in memory, or high-entropy sections, signal packing/compression); and the Import Address Table (IAT) lists the API functions the malware calls, which strongly hints at capability: imports of network APIs suggest communication, cryptographic APIs suggest ransomware or config encryption, process-injection APIs suggest stealth, and registry APIs suggest persistence. A sample that imports almost nothing is itself suspicious — it is likely packed, resolving its real imports only at runtime to hide them from static view. Reading the PE structure, imports, and section characteristics lets an analyst form a strong hypothesis about what a sample does and whether it is obfuscated, all before a single instruction executes — directing where the deeper dynamic and disassembly effort should go."],
ex:"Triaging an unknown binary, an analyst finds it is a PE despite a .pdf extension, computes its SHA-256 (unknown to threat intel — likely fresh), and pulls strings revealing a suspicious domain and a mutex name. The PE shows only three imports and a high-entropy section — classic packing. From triage alone she concludes: a freshly built, packed sample with network capability, and she prioritizes unpacking it dynamically.",
refs:[["PE file format reference","Headers, sections, and the import table."],["Static triage tooling (PEStudio, strings, hashing)","First-pass characterization."]],
kc:[
{q:"Extracting strings from a sample often reveals:",o:["Nothing useful","URLs, IPs, file paths, registry keys, and command strings — early clues to behavior","Only random noise","The attacker's name always"],a:1,x:"Strings frequently expose C2 domains, paths, registry keys, and commands — fast, safe early intelligence."},
{q:"The Import Address Table (IAT) hints at capability because:",o:["It stores passwords","The APIs a sample imports (network, crypto, injection, registry) suggest what it does","It is decorative","It hides the file"],a:1,x:"Imported APIs map to capability — network APIs mean communication, crypto means encryption/ransomware, and so on."},
{q:"A sample that imports almost nothing is suspicious because:",o:["It is safe","It is likely packed — resolving real imports only at runtime to hide them from static view","It does nothing","It is small"],a:1,x:"Near-empty imports signal packing; the malware hides its true API calls until it unpacks at runtime."}]},
{t:"Assembly and CPU Architecture for RE",d:"The language the machine actually runs.",
obj:["Explain the x86/x64 register and memory model","Read common assembly instructions and control flow","Map high-level constructs to their assembly forms"],
read:["Reverse engineering compiled malware requires reading assembly — the low-level instructions the CPU executes — because the source code is gone and the binary is all you have. The x86/x64 architecture is register-based: a small set of fast CPU registers (general-purpose registers like EAX/RAX, ECX, EDX; the stack pointer ESP/RSP and base pointer EBP/RBP; the instruction pointer EIP/RIP that tracks execution) hold working values, while memory holds the rest. Instructions move data (MOV), do arithmetic and logic (ADD, SUB, XOR, AND), manipulate the stack (PUSH, POP), and control flow (JMP for unconditional jumps, conditional jumps like JE/JNE based on flags set by CMP/TEST, CALL and RET for functions). Understanding the register model and the stack — how arguments and local variables live on the stack, how CALL pushes a return address and RET pops it — is the vocabulary of everything that follows.",
"The analyst's real skill is pattern recognition: mapping assembly back to the high-level constructs the compiler produced. A CMP followed by a conditional jump is an if statement; a backward jump to a compared counter is a loop; a sequence of PUSH instructions before a CALL is argument passing; a function prologue (PUSH EBP; MOV EBP, ESP) and epilogue mark function boundaries; an XOR of a register with itself is a fast zero. Calling conventions (how arguments are passed — on the stack in 32-bit stdcall/cdecl, in registers in 64-bit) tell you how to read function calls and their parameters, which is essential when the parameters are the C2 address or the encryption key you are hunting for. You do not need to write assembly to reverse malware, but you must read it fluently enough to follow the logic, recognize the constructs, and find the handful of instructions that matter — the compare that gates the payload, the loop that decrypts the config, the call that sends data to the C2."],
ex:"Reading a decompiled routine, an analyst recognizes a function prologue, a loop that XORs each byte of a buffer against a rotating key, and a CALL to a network-send function immediately after. From the assembly alone she identifies the pattern: the malware decrypts an embedded blob with a simple XOR key and then transmits it — the decrypt-then-exfiltrate logic she needs to extract the config.",
refs:[["x86/x64 assembly and calling conventions reference","Registers, stack, and instruction set."],["Recognizing compiler-generated constructs in assembly","Mapping asm back to source logic."]],
kc:[
{q:"In x86/x64, general-purpose registers are used to:",o:["Store files","Hold fast working values the CPU operates on, alongside the stack and memory","Encrypt disks","Nothing"],a:1,x:"Registers hold the CPU's fast working values; reading them is central to following what the code does."},
{q:"A CMP followed by a conditional jump usually represents:",o:["A loop only","An if/branch decision — the compare sets flags the jump tests","Encryption","A function call"],a:1,x:"CMP sets flags and the conditional jump branches on them — the assembly form of an if statement."},
{q:"Why must an analyst read assembly fluently?",o:["To write malware","To follow the logic and find the few instructions that matter — the config decrypt, the C2 call","It is optional trivia","To slow down"],a:1,x:"Fluent reading lets the analyst follow logic and locate the key instructions (decrypt loop, C2 send) that yield intelligence."}]},
{t:"Disassembly and Analysis Tools",d:"Turning bytes into readable code with IDA and Ghidra.",
obj:["Explain how disassemblers and decompilers work","Navigate a binary with Ghidra/IDA effectively","Use cross-references and function graphs to find key logic"],
read:["Disassemblers translate a binary's raw bytes back into human-readable assembly, and modern tools (IDA Pro, and the free, powerful Ghidra) add a decompiler that further lifts assembly into C-like pseudocode, dramatically speeding analysis. These tools do far more than translate: they identify functions, reconstruct control-flow graphs (visual maps of a function's branches and loops), label and track cross-references (every place a function, string, or address is used), and let the analyst rename variables and functions as understanding grows, turning an opaque binary into an annotated, navigable map. Ghidra's decompiler output — readable pseudocode next to the assembly — lets an analyst reason about logic at a higher level while dropping to assembly for the details, and its free availability has made professional-grade reverse engineering broadly accessible.",
"Effective navigation is a skill in itself. Rather than reading a binary top to bottom, analysts work from anchors: interesting strings (the C2 domain) and their cross-references lead to the code that uses them; imported APIs (a network-send call, a crypto call) and their cross-references lead to the functions that matter; the entry point and the function-call graph reveal structure. Cross-references are the analyst's most powerful tool — finding every place that references the suspicious string or the decryption function collapses a huge binary down to the handful of relevant functions. The analyst iteratively renames functions and variables as their purpose becomes clear (a function that resolves APIs by hash becomes resolve_apis; a buffer that holds the decrypted config becomes config_blob), building an increasingly readable model. Mastery of the disassembler — its navigation, cross-references, graphs, and annotation — is what makes reversing a large real-world sample tractable rather than overwhelming."],
ex:"Loading a sample into Ghidra, an analyst does not read it linearly. She locates the one interesting string — a URL fragment — checks its cross-references to find the single function that uses it, and reads that function's decompiled pseudocode. It builds an HTTP request and calls send. In minutes, using strings-plus-cross-references, she has found the C2 communication routine inside a 200-function binary.",
refs:[["Ghidra documentation and reverse-engineering workflow","Disassembly, decompilation, and navigation."],["Using cross-references and call graphs in IDA/Ghidra","Anchoring analysis on strings and imports."]],
kc:[
{q:"A decompiler (as in Ghidra) helps by:",o:["Running the malware","Lifting assembly into C-like pseudocode, so the analyst reasons at a higher level","Encrypting the binary","Deleting the sample"],a:1,x:"Decompilers produce readable pseudocode alongside assembly, speeding reasoning about logic."},
{q:"Cross-references are powerful because they:",o:["Hide code","Find every place a string, function, or address is used — collapsing a huge binary to the relevant functions","Run the sample","Do nothing"],a:1,x:"Following cross-references from a suspicious string or API to its callers pinpoints the code that matters."},
{q:"An effective analyst navigates a binary by:",o:["Reading it top to bottom","Anchoring on interesting strings and imports and following their cross-references","Guessing randomly","Never using the tool"],a:1,x:"Working from anchors (strings, imports) and their cross-references is far faster than linear reading."}]},
{t:"Interactive Debugging",d:"Driving execution one instruction at a time.",
obj:["Explain the role of a debugger in malware analysis","Use breakpoints, stepping, and memory inspection effectively","Combine debugging with disassembly to confirm behavior"],
read:["A debugger lets the analyst control a sample's execution interactively — pausing at chosen points, stepping through instructions one at a time, inspecting and modifying registers and memory, and watching the malware's logic unfold live — which complements the static picture the disassembler provides. Where the disassembler shows what the code could do, the debugger shows what it does do with real data: the actual value in a register at a branch, the decrypted buffer after a loop runs, the address the malware jumps to after unpacking. User-mode debuggers (x64dbg for user-mode Windows malware, and WinDbg for kernel and deeper work) are the analyst's interactive workbench, and debugging is often the fastest way to answer a specific question — what does this function return, what does this loop produce, where does control go after this check — that would be laborious to reason out purely statically.",
"The core debugging skills are breakpoints, stepping, and inspection. Breakpoints pause execution at an address, an API call (break when the malware calls a network or crypto function to catch it in the act), or a memory access (break when a buffer is read or written — invaluable for catching the moment a config is decrypted). Stepping (step-over to run a call as a unit, step-into to enter it, step-out to return) walks the analyst through the logic at the right granularity. Inspection reads and modifies registers and memory, letting the analyst see the decrypted data, dump an unpacked payload (module eight), or patch a value to force a path (module eight's anti-analysis defeat). The most productive analysis interleaves debugger and disassembler: reading the disassembly to plan where to break, then debugging to confirm the runtime reality and capture the data the static view cannot compute. Interactive debugging turns the analyst from a reader of code into an operator of it, and is indispensable for unpacking, defeating evasion, and extracting the runtime-only data — decrypted configs, resolved APIs, in-memory payloads — that hold the intelligence."],
ex:"To find where a sample decrypts its config, the analyst sets a memory breakpoint on the config buffer and runs. The debugger pauses the instant the decryption loop finishes writing, and she reads the now-plaintext config directly from memory — the C2 IPs and settings appear without her having to reverse the decryption math by hand. The breakpoint caught the data at exactly the moment it existed in the clear.",
refs:[["Interactive debugging with x64dbg/WinDbg","Breakpoints, stepping, and memory inspection."],["Combining static and dynamic analysis","Interleaving disassembler and debugger."]],
kc:[
{q:"A debugger complements the disassembler by showing:",o:["Nothing new","What the code actually does with real data — register values, decrypted buffers, and taken branches at runtime","Only the file size","The author's name"],a:1,x:"The disassembler shows possible behavior; the debugger shows the runtime reality and the data the static view cannot compute."},
{q:"A memory breakpoint is invaluable for:",o:["Deleting memory","Catching the moment a buffer is read/written — e.g., pausing right when a config is decrypted","Encrypting the sample","Slowing the CPU"],a:1,x:"Breaking on a memory access catches the exact instant data (like a decrypted config) exists in the clear."},
{q:"The most productive analysis:",o:["Uses only the debugger","Interleaves disassembler (to plan where to break) and debugger (to confirm runtime and capture data)","Avoids both","Uses only static reading"],a:1,x:"Reading disassembly to plan breakpoints, then debugging to confirm and capture runtime data, is the powerful combined workflow."}]},
{t:"Dynamic Analysis and Sandboxing",d:"Watching malware run in a controlled cage.",
obj:["Explain the value and risks of detonating malware","Use sandboxes and monitoring tools to observe behavior","Interpret automated sandbox reports critically"],
read:["Dynamic analysis executes the sample in a controlled, instrumented environment and observes what it actually does — often the fastest way to understand behavior that static analysis obscures, especially for packed or obfuscated samples that only reveal their true code and network activity at runtime. The controlled environment (a monitored VM, or an automated sandbox like Cuckoo/CAPE or a commercial service) records the sample's actions: processes it creates, files it writes, registry keys it sets, mutexes it creates (often used to prevent re-infection and useful as host IOCs), and — critically — the network connections it attempts. Running the sample and watching it reach out to a C2 domain, drop a payload, and establish persistence can reveal in seconds what would take hours to reconstruct statically. Detonation is powerful precisely because the malware does the unpacking and decryption for you.",
"Dynamic analysis demands both safety and skepticism. Safety comes from the isolated lab (module one) with snapshots and controlled/simulated internet, so the detonation is observed without giving a real adversary a live victim or letting the sample escape. Skepticism is needed because sophisticated malware detects analysis environments (module eight) and behaves benignly when it senses a VM, a sandbox, or an analyst — so a clean automated-sandbox report does not prove a sample is safe; it may simply mean the sample refused to run its real payload. Analysts therefore treat automated reports as leads, not verdicts, correlate dynamic observations with static findings, and manually detonate with debuggers and monitoring tools when the automated run is evasive or incomplete. Well-run dynamic analysis — safe detonation, careful behavioral capture, and critical interpretation — turns the malware's own execution into the clearest evidence of its capabilities and the richest source of IOCs."],
ex:"An analyst detonates a packed sample in an instrumented VM pointed at a simulated internet. Within seconds the process-monitor shows it unpacking a second stage into memory, creating a mutex, writing a run-key for persistence, and beaconing to a domain. The dynamic run hands her the unpacked payload, the persistence mechanism, and the C2 — behaviors the packing had hidden from static view.",
refs:[["Automated sandbox analysis (Cuckoo/CAPE) and monitoring tools","Behavioral capture and reporting."],["Interpreting sandbox reports critically","Why a clean run is not proof of safety."]],
kc:[
{q:"Dynamic analysis is especially valuable for packed samples because:",o:["It is slower","The malware unpacks and decrypts itself at runtime, revealing behavior static analysis hides","It cannot see anything","Packing prevents it"],a:1,x:"Running a packed sample makes it reveal its true code and network activity — the malware does the unpacking for you."},
{q:"A clean automated-sandbox report means:",o:["The sample is definitely safe","Not necessarily — the sample may have detected the sandbox and refused to run its real payload","The analysis is over","Malware is impossible"],a:1,x:"Evasive malware behaves benignly when it detects analysis; a clean run is a lead, not a verdict."},
{q:"Safe detonation requires:",o:["A production network","An isolated lab with snapshots and controlled/simulated internet","No precautions","Running on your laptop"],a:1,x:"Isolation, snapshots, and controlled internet let you observe execution without causing a real incident or leaking to the adversary."}]},
{t:"Behavioral Analysis: Host and Process",d:"Reading the footprints malware leaves on a system.",
obj:["Trace process, file, and registry activity of a running sample","Identify persistence and privilege mechanisms","Derive host-based IOCs from behavior"],
read:["Behavioral analysis on the host studies the footprints a running sample leaves: the processes it spawns, the files it drops, the registry keys it modifies, the services it installs, and the mechanisms it uses to survive a reboot and to elevate privileges. Process activity reveals structure — a document dropping and launching a child process, a benign-looking process hollowed out and replaced (process hollowing), or one process injecting code into another (module eleven) all show in a process monitor as parent-child relationships and unusual behavior. File activity shows dropped payloads and staging locations; registry activity shows configuration storage and, importantly, persistence: the Run keys, scheduled tasks, services, and startup entries malware uses to reload after reboot. Each of these observations is both an understanding of the malware and a concrete host-based indicator a defender can hunt for across the fleet.",
"Persistence and privilege are the behaviors defenders most need to enumerate, because they determine remediation. To fully clean an infection you must find every persistence mechanism — a missed scheduled task or service means the malware returns. Behavioral analysis maps these: the ATT&CK Persistence and Privilege Escalation techniques (registry run keys, scheduled tasks, services, WMI subscriptions, DLL hijacking) each leave observable host artifacts, and cataloguing them turns analysis into an actionable remediation checklist. The output of host behavioral analysis is a set of durable IOCs and remediation steps: the exact registry keys, file paths, service names, mutexes, and scheduled tasks the malware creates, which detection engineers turn into hunting queries and responders use to scope and eradicate the infection. This translation — from observed behavior to host IOCs and a complete remediation list — is where behavioral analysis directly protects the organization."],
ex:"Watching a sample execute, an analyst records: it drops a DLL to a hidden AppData folder, registers it as a scheduled task and a run-key (two persistence mechanisms), and creates a named mutex. She turns these into host IOCs and a remediation checklist — both persistence entries and the dropped file must be removed, or the malware reloads. Missing either would leave the host reinfected.",
refs:[["Process/registry/file monitoring (Procmon, Autoruns)","Capturing host behavior."],["MITRE ATT&CK Persistence and Privilege Escalation","Cataloguing survival mechanisms."]],
kc:[
{q:"Enumerating every persistence mechanism matters because:",o:["It is optional","A missed scheduled task or service means the malware reloads after cleanup — remediation stays incomplete","Persistence is harmless","It slows analysis"],a:1,x:"Complete remediation requires finding all persistence; one missed mechanism reinfects the host."},
{q:"Host behavioral analysis produces:",o:["Only curiosity","Durable host IOCs (keys, paths, services, mutexes) and a remediation checklist defenders act on","Nothing usable","Offensive tools"],a:1,x:"Observed behavior becomes concrete host indicators and remediation steps — the defensive payoff."},
{q:"A named mutex created by malware is useful to defenders as:",o:["An encryption key","A host IOC — often used to prevent re-infection and detectable across the fleet","A network port","Irrelevant"],a:1,x:"Mutex names are stable host indicators that can be hunted across systems to find infections."}]},
{t:"Unpacking and Deobfuscation",d:"Peeling away the layers that hide the real code.",
obj:["Recognize packing and its indicators","Apply manual and automated unpacking approaches","Deobfuscate strings and control flow to reveal logic"],
read:["Packing and obfuscation are how malware hides its real code from static analysis. A packer compresses or encrypts the true payload and wraps it in a small stub that, at runtime, unpacks the payload into memory and jumps to it — so the on-disk file reveals almost nothing (few imports, high-entropy sections, a tiny stub), and only when it runs does the real code appear. Recognizing packing (the module-two indicators: sparse imports, high entropy, sections that expand in memory, known packer signatures) tells the analyst that static analysis alone will not suffice. The most reliable unpacking approach leverages dynamic analysis: let the malware unpack itself by running it under a debugger, set a breakpoint at the moment it transfers control to the freshly unpacked code (the tail jump / OEP — original entry point), and then dump the unpacked payload from memory for static analysis. The malware, in the act of running, does the decryption for you — you simply capture the result.",
"Beyond whole-file packing, malware obfuscates in finer-grained ways that must be undone to understand it: strings are encrypted or built at runtime (so the C2 domain never appears as a plain string on disk), APIs are resolved dynamically by hash (so the import table hides the malware's true capabilities), and control flow is flattened or junk-filled to confuse disassemblers. Deobfuscation reverses these: recognizing and replicating the string-decryption routine to recover the hidden strings (often revealing the config, C2, and commands), resolving the API hashes back to function names to expose capability, and mentally or scriptedly stripping junk code and flattened control flow to see the real logic. Analysts frequently write small scripts (in the disassembler's scripting interface or standalone) that replicate the malware's own decryption algorithm to bulk-recover its obfuscated strings — turning an opaque sample into a readable one. Unpacking and deobfuscation are the gate between a sample that tells you nothing and one that hands you its config, C2, and behavior; defeating them is often the single most valuable step in an analysis."],
ex:"A packed sample shows only a stub statically. The analyst runs it under a debugger, breaks at the tail jump to the unpacked code, and dumps the payload from memory — now fully analyzable. Inside, strings are still encrypted; she identifies the one-line XOR string-decrypt routine, scripts it, and bulk-decrypts the strings, recovering the C2 domain and command keywords the packing had hidden.",
refs:[["Manual unpacking with debuggers (finding the OEP)","Dumping unpacked payloads from memory."],["String and API deobfuscation techniques","Recovering hidden strings and resolving API hashes."]],
kc:[
{q:"The most reliable way to unpack a sample is often to:",o:["Guess the algorithm","Let it unpack itself under a debugger, break at the tail jump to the OEP, and dump the payload from memory","Delete the packer","Ignore it"],a:1,x:"Running the malware makes it decrypt its own payload; the analyst breaks at the OEP and dumps the unpacked code."},
{q:"Malware resolves APIs dynamically by hash in order to:",o:["Run faster only","Hide its true capabilities from the static import table","Improve compatibility","Help analysts"],a:1,x:"Hash-based API resolution keeps the import table empty of revealing functions, hiding capability from static view."},
{q:"Analysts often script the malware's own string-decrypt routine to:",o:["Encrypt more","Bulk-recover its obfuscated strings — revealing config, C2, and commands","Delete strings","Slow down"],a:1,x:"Replicating the sample's decryption algorithm recovers its hidden strings in bulk, exposing the key intelligence."}]},
{t:"Anti-Analysis and Evasion Techniques",d:"How malware fights back, and how analysts win anyway.",
obj:["Enumerate anti-VM, anti-debug, and anti-sandbox techniques","Explain environment-aware and time-based evasion","Apply countermeasures to defeat evasion"],
read:["Sophisticated malware assumes it will be analyzed and actively resists it. Anti-debugging techniques detect a debugger (checking the PEB BeingDebugged flag, timing checks that notice single-stepping's slowness, exceptions that behave differently under a debugger) and alter behavior or crash when one is present. Anti-VM and anti-sandbox techniques detect analysis environments by their tells: known VM artifacts (drivers, MAC-address prefixes, registry keys), a suspiciously small disk or few CPU cores, the absence of normal user activity (no mouse movement, no browsing history, a freshly booted system), or the presence of analysis tools. Environment-aware malware checks these signals and, if it concludes it is being watched, refuses to run its real payload — behaving benignly so the analyst concludes it is harmless. This is why a clean sandbox run proves nothing (module five): the malware may simply have detected the sandbox.",
"Time and logic-based evasion add further obstacles: malware may sleep for long periods or wait for a specific date, a certain number of reboots, a particular domain-joined environment, or a targeting condition (a specific language setting, a specific company's domain) before activating — so a short automated run sees nothing, and only the intended victim triggers the payload. Analysts defeat these techniques with a countermeasure toolkit: anti-anti-debugging plugins that hide the debugger, VM-hardening that removes obvious virtualization tells and simulates a used, human-operated system, patching or forcing the evasion checks (setting the BeingDebugged flag to zero, patching a jump so the payload runs regardless), fast-forwarding or patching out sleep calls, and feeding the malware the environment it demands. The analytical mindset is adversarial: recognize that the sample is trying to hide, find the checks that gate its real behavior, and neutralize them so the payload runs and reveals itself. Defeating anti-analysis is often what separates a shallow report from a complete one — the real capabilities live behind the checks the malware hoped would stop you."],
ex:"A sample runs benignly in the sandbox. The analyst suspects evasion, loads it in a debugger, and finds an early check for VM artifacts and a BeingDebugged test that, when either is true, jumps straight to a clean exit. She hardens the VM, patches the debugger check, and forces the jump the other way — the sample now unpacks and beacons, revealing the payload its evasion had concealed.",
refs:[["Anti-debugging and anti-VM technique catalogs","How malware detects analysis."],["Countermeasures: VM hardening and anti-anti-debug","Forcing evasive samples to run."]],
kc:[
{q:"Environment-aware malware behaves benignly when it detects analysis in order to:",o:["Help the analyst","Fool the analyst into concluding it is harmless, hiding its real payload","Run faster","Save memory"],a:1,x:"By acting benign under analysis, evasive malware misleads the analyst — which is why a clean run is not proof of safety."},
{q:"Time-based evasion (long sleeps, specific dates) defeats:",o:["Nothing","Short automated runs — which see no malicious behavior in the observation window","Static analysis only","The CPU"],a:1,x:"If the payload waits for a date or a long delay, a brief sandbox run observes nothing; only the intended trigger activates it."},
{q:"Analysts defeat anti-analysis by:",o:["Giving up","Hardening the VM, hiding the debugger, and patching/forcing the evasion checks so the real payload runs","Running once and quitting","Trusting the clean run"],a:1,x:"The countermeasure toolkit neutralizes the checks that gate the payload, forcing the malware to reveal its real behavior."}]},
{t:"Extracting Configurations and IOCs",d:"Pulling the actionable intelligence out of a sample.",
obj:["Locate and decode embedded malware configurations","Extract C2 addresses, keys, and campaign identifiers","Produce structured IOCs for detection and sharing"],
read:["The configuration block is the heart of many malware families and the richest source of intelligence — a structured blob, usually encrypted or encoded and embedded in the sample, that holds the operational parameters: the C2 servers (domains and target IPs), communication settings (ports, URIs, user-agents, beacon intervals), encryption keys, a campaign or botnet identifier that ties the sample to a specific operation, and feature flags. Families like many RATs and loaders store their config in a predictable, decodable format, and once an analyst locates and decodes it, the sample surrenders the exact indicators defenders need. Extracting the config is frequently the single highest-value output of an analysis, because the C2 addresses become network detections and blocks, the campaign ID enables tracking the adversary across samples, and the settings inform detection rules — turning one analyzed sample into fleet-wide and even community-wide protection.",
"Extraction follows from the earlier skills: the analyst finds where the config is stored (often referenced right before the malware first uses its settings — for example, just before it builds its first C2 request), identifies the decoding routine (the same string/blob-decryption logic from module eight — an XOR, RC4, AES, or custom scheme), and decodes the block to recover its fields. Mature analysis then produces structured, shareable output: IOCs formatted for automation and sharing (STIX/TAXII, or detection formats like YARA for file identification and Snort/Suricata for network detection), so the extracted C2 IPs and domains flow into blocklists and IDS, the file characteristics become YARA rules that find related samples, and the whole package can be shared with the community. The discipline is to move from a decoded config to operational defenses: every extracted target IP, domain, key, and campaign ID should end up in a detection, a block, an intelligence record, or a shared indicator — the concrete realization of the course's promise that analysis produces protection. This is also precisely the capstone task: reverse a sample to extract its config blocks, target IPs, and communication mechanisms."],
ex:"Analyzing a RAT, the analyst locates the config referenced just before the first network call, recognizes an RC4-encoded blob, and decodes it with the key found nearby. Out come three C2 IP addresses, the beacon URI and interval, and a campaign ID. She pushes the IPs to the firewall blocklist and IDS, writes a YARA rule from the sample's structure, and files the campaign ID in threat intel — one sample yielding fleet-wide detection.",
refs:[["Malware config extraction methodology","Locating and decoding embedded configurations."],["YARA and network-signature authoring","Turning IOCs into detections."]],
kc:[
{q:"A malware configuration block typically contains:",o:["User documents","C2 servers/target IPs, communication settings, keys, and a campaign identifier","The operating system","Nothing"],a:1,x:"The config holds the operational parameters — C2 addresses, comms settings, keys, and campaign ID — the core intelligence."},
{q:"Extracting the config is high-value because:",o:["It is hard","Its C2 addresses become network blocks/detections and its campaign ID enables tracking the adversary across samples","It looks impressive","It is meaningless"],a:1,x:"The decoded config turns one sample into fleet-wide detections, blocks, and cross-sample adversary tracking."},
{q:"Structured IOC formats (YARA, Snort/Suricata, STIX) exist to:",o:["Slow analysis","Turn extracted indicators into automated detections and shareable intelligence","Hide the config","Encrypt malware"],a:1,x:"These formats operationalize IOCs into file/network detections and community sharing — analysis becoming protection."}]},
{t:"Network Communication and C2 Analysis",d:"Decoding how malware talks to its operators.",
obj:["Analyze malware network protocols and beaconing","Decode encrypted or encoded C2 traffic","Derive network detections from communication patterns"],
read:["Malware's network communication — how it beacons to its command-and-control (C2) infrastructure, receives instructions, and exfiltrates data — is both a core capability to understand and a rich source of network detections. Analysis characterizes the communication: the protocol (HTTP/HTTPS is most common for blending with normal traffic, but also DNS tunneling, custom TCP, and increasingly cloud/legitimate services abused as C2), the beaconing pattern (regular intervals, often with jitter to evade detection), the request structure (URIs, user-agents, headers, and how data is encoded into requests), and the encryption or encoding protecting the content. Capturing the traffic in the lab (module five's controlled network) and correlating it with the code that generates it (the C2 routine found via cross-references in module four) reveals exactly how the malware talks — which becomes network signatures, blocklists, and hunting queries.",
"Decoding C2 content is where reverse engineering and network analysis meet. Malware rarely sends plaintext; it encrypts or encodes its beacons and commands, often with the same scheme used for its config (module nine). By reversing the communication routine, the analyst learns how requests are built and how responses are parsed, recovers the keys, and can then decode captured traffic to read the actual commands the operator sent and the data the malware exfiltrated — turning opaque packets into a transcript of the intrusion. The defensive outputs are substantial: the C2 domains and IPs become blocks; the beaconing pattern, URI structure, user-agent, and JA3/TLS fingerprint become network detection signatures (Snort/Suricata rules) that catch the family even on new infrastructure; and the understanding of the protocol supports emulation for tracking and takedown. Even when content is fully encrypted, the metadata — timing, sizes, endpoints, TLS characteristics — yields robust detections. Network C2 analysis converts the malware's need to communicate into the defender's opportunity to detect, block, and understand the intrusion."],
ex:"Capturing a sample's traffic, the analyst sees regular HTTPS beacons with a distinctive URI pattern and user-agent. Reversing the C2 routine, she recovers the RC4 key the malware uses, decodes a captured response, and reads the operator's command to enumerate files. She writes a Suricata rule on the URI/user-agent pattern that detects the family regardless of C2 IP — a durable network detection born from the communication analysis.",
refs:[["Malware C2 protocol analysis and traffic capture","Beaconing, encoding, and decoding communications."],["Network signature authoring for C2 detection","URI, user-agent, and TLS-fingerprint rules."]],
kc:[
{q:"Malware favors HTTP/HTTPS for C2 mainly to:",o:["Be slow","Blend with normal web traffic and pass through firewalls","Be detected","Save power"],a:1,x:"HTTP/HTTPS blends with legitimate traffic and traverses firewalls, making it the common C2 channel."},
{q:"Reversing the C2 routine lets an analyst:",o:["Nothing new","Recover the keys and decode captured traffic — reading the actual commands and exfiltrated data","Only crash the sample","Encrypt traffic"],a:1,x:"Understanding how comms are built and keyed lets the analyst decrypt captured C2 into a readable transcript of the intrusion."},
{q:"Even with fully encrypted content, robust detections can come from:",o:["Nothing","Metadata — timing/beacon pattern, URI structure, user-agent, and TLS fingerprint","Guesswork","The ransom note"],a:1,x:"Traffic metadata (beacon timing, URI, user-agent, TLS/JA3) yields signatures even when the payload is encrypted."}]},
{t:"Shellcode, Injection, and In-Memory Techniques",d:"How malware runs stealthily inside other processes.",
obj:["Explain shellcode and position-independent code","Describe process injection and hollowing techniques","Analyze in-memory and fileless malware"],
read:["Modern malware increasingly runs in memory rather than as a file on disk, to evade file-based defenses, and analysts must understand the techniques. Shellcode is small, position-independent code (it runs correctly regardless of where it is loaded, because it resolves its own APIs — often by hash, module eight — and uses relative addressing) that is frequently the initial payload delivered by an exploit or loader; analyzing it requires recognizing its self-contained, API-resolving nature and often emulating or debugging it to see what it does. Process injection is the family of techniques by which malware runs its code inside another, legitimate process to hide: classic DLL injection, hollowing (creating a legitimate process suspended, unmapping its real code, and replacing it with malicious code so the process looks legitimate but runs malware), and more advanced approaches (thread hijacking, APC injection, and living off the land by injecting into trusted processes). These leave characteristic traces — a process whose in-memory code does not match its on-disk image, unexpected executable memory regions, or one process writing into another's memory.",
"Fileless and in-memory malware pushes this further: the payload may never touch disk, living only in memory, in the registry, or in WMI, and executing through legitimate tools (PowerShell, WMI, LOLBins — living-off-the-land binaries). Analyzing these requires memory forensics — capturing and examining process memory to find the injected code, the unpacked payload, and the injected regions that dynamic behavioral analysis flagged. The analyst extracts the in-memory payload for static analysis (the same dump-from-memory approach as unpacking, module eight), reconstructs what the injected code does, and identifies the injection technique (which maps to ATT&CK and to specific detection opportunities — memory-integrity checks, injection-behavior detections, and monitoring of the abused legitimate tools). Understanding shellcode, injection, and fileless execution is essential for analyzing sophisticated and nation-state malware (module fourteen), which relies heavily on these stealth techniques, and it directly informs the detection strategy: since these threats evade file scanning, defenders detect them through behavior and memory rather than files — a shift the analysis makes concrete."],
ex:"Behavioral analysis showed a benign-looking process beaconing to a C2, though its on-disk file was clean. The analyst captures the process's memory and finds an injected executable region whose code does not match the disk image — process hollowing. She dumps the injected payload, analyzes it as the real malware, and recommends detection via memory-injection monitoring rather than file scanning, which the fileless technique defeats.",
refs:[["Process injection and hollowing techniques (ATT&CK)","How malware runs inside legitimate processes."],["Memory forensics for injected and fileless malware","Extracting in-memory payloads."]],
kc:[
{q:"Shellcode is position-independent, meaning it:",o:["Only runs at a fixed address","Runs correctly wherever it is loaded — resolving its own APIs and using relative addressing","Cannot run","Needs an installer"],a:1,x:"Position-independent shellcode resolves its own APIs and uses relative addressing, so it runs regardless of load location."},
{q:"Process hollowing hides malware by:",o:["Deleting the process","Replacing a legitimate suspended process's code with malicious code, so it looks legitimate but runs malware","Encrypting the disk","Doing nothing"],a:1,x:"Hollowing swaps a legitimate process's in-memory code for malicious code — the process appears normal but executes malware."},
{q:"Fileless malware is best detected through:",o:["File scanning","Behavior and memory analysis — since the payload may never touch disk","Ignoring it","Antivirus signatures on files"],a:1,x:"Fileless threats evade file scanning; behavior- and memory-based detection is the effective approach."}]},
{t:"Document, Script, and Macro-Based Malware",d:"Analyzing the delivery vehicles that start most intrusions.",
obj:["Analyze malicious documents and their macros","Deobfuscate malicious scripts (PowerShell, JS, VBA)","Trace multi-stage delivery chains"],
read:["Most intrusions begin not with an executable but with a document or script — a phishing attachment (a Word doc with a malicious macro, an Excel 4.0 macro sheet, a PDF, a OneNote file) or a script (JavaScript, VBScript, PowerShell, HTA) — and analyzing these delivery vehicles is essential for understanding and detecting the initial-access stage. Malicious Office documents typically hide code in macros (VBA) or increasingly in newer techniques, and analysis extracts the embedded macro/script, deobfuscates it (these are heavily obfuscated — string concatenation, encoding, character-code arithmetic to hide URLs and commands), and reads what it does: usually it downloads and executes a next-stage payload. Tools that parse Office structures and extract macros, combined with careful manual deobfuscation, reveal the delivery logic — the URL the document reaches out to, the payload it drops, and how it executes it — which are exactly the IOCs needed to detect and block the campaign at the point of entry.",
"Script-based malware, especially PowerShell, dominates modern delivery and fileless execution because it is powerful, native to Windows, and easily obfuscated. Malicious scripts obfuscate aggressively — Base64-encoded commands, string manipulation, compression, and layered encoding — and the analyst's job is to peel the layers: decode the Base64, reverse the string tricks, and expand each stage until the real behavior is visible (typically downloading a payload, injecting shellcode, or establishing persistence). Multi-stage chains are common — a macro launches an obfuscated PowerShell command that downloads a second script that fetches the final payload — and tracing the full chain reveals every stage's URL and behavior, giving defenders indicators and detection opportunities at each hop. Because these techniques abuse legitimate interpreters and often run filelessly (module twelve), detection focuses on the behavior: suspicious parent-child relationships (Office spawning PowerShell), encoded command lines, and script-block logging. Analyzing document and script malware protects the organization at the earliest, cheapest point to stop an intrusion — before the executable payload ever lands."],
ex:"A phishing Word document is analyzed: its VBA macro is extracted and deobfuscated, revealing a call that launches an encoded PowerShell command. Decoding the Base64 shows a downloader reaching out to a URL for a second stage. The analyst maps the full chain (doc → PowerShell → download → payload), extracts every URL as an IOC, and recommends detecting Office-spawns-PowerShell — catching the campaign at initial access.",
refs:[["Malicious Office document and macro analysis (oletools)","Extracting and reading embedded macros."],["PowerShell/JS deobfuscation and multi-stage delivery","Peeling layered obfuscation."]],
kc:[
{q:"Most intrusions begin with:",o:["An executable directly","A document or script delivery vehicle — a phishing attachment or malicious script","A firewall","Nothing"],a:1,x:"Documents and scripts (phishing attachments, macros, PowerShell) are the common initial-access vehicles."},
{q:"Malicious PowerShell is analyzed by:",o:["Running it blindly","Peeling the obfuscation layers — decoding Base64 and reversing string tricks — to reveal the real behavior","Deleting PowerShell","Ignoring it"],a:1,x:"Malicious scripts layer encoding and string manipulation; the analyst decodes each layer to expose the true logic."},
{q:"A strong behavioral detection for document malware is:",o:["Blocking all documents","Office spawning PowerShell (or other interpreters) — a suspicious parent-child relationship","Faster CPUs","Nothing"],a:1,x:"Office applications launching script interpreters is a high-signal behavioral indicator of macro-based delivery."}]},
{t:"APTs and Nation-State Toolkits",d:"Dissecting the most sophisticated adversaries.",
obj:["Characterize advanced persistent threat tradecraft","Analyze modular, multi-component toolkits","Extract intelligence supporting attribution and tracking"],
read:["Advanced persistent threats (APTs) — typically well-resourced, often nation-state, adversaries pursuing long-term espionage or strategic goals — build malware that concentrates every technique in this course, and analyzing their toolkits is the discipline's most demanding and highest-stakes work. APT malware is characteristically modular: rather than a single executable, it is a framework of components — a small, stealthy initial implant that establishes a foothold, downloadable modules for specific tasks (keylogging, credential theft, lateral movement, exfiltration), and sophisticated C2 — so analysis must piece together a system, not just a file. It employs heavy anti-analysis (module eight), custom encryption and packing, fileless and in-memory techniques (module twelve), living-off-the-land, and careful operational security to remain undetected for months or years. The analyst confronts advanced obfuscation, novel techniques, and deliberate misdirection, and must apply the full toolkit patiently to understand a component's role in the larger operation.",
"Beyond capability, APT analysis produces strategic intelligence and supports attribution — carefully. Analysts extract the tradecraft (the specific techniques, mapped to ATT&CK, that characterize the group), infrastructure (C2 domains, IPs, and patterns), code artifacts (shared code, compilation timestamps, language and toolmarks, reused encryption schemes or config formats that link samples to a known toolkit or group), and targeting (who and what the malware seeks), building a picture that ties the sample to a tracked adversary and informs strategic defense. Attribution is approached with rigor and humility: technical indicators can be shared, reused, or deliberately faked (false flags), so responsible analysis distinguishes what the evidence supports (this sample shares code and infrastructure with a tracked cluster) from unsupported leaps (naming a specific government), and defers definitive attribution to the fuller intelligence picture. The defensive value is immense — understanding an APT toolkit yields the detections, hunting hypotheses, and strategic warning to defend against a determined adversary — but the analyst's duty is to produce accurate, evidence-bounded intelligence, not sensational claims. Dissecting nation-state toolkits is the culmination of the analytical skillset in service of defending against the adversaries that matter most."],
ex:"Analyzing a stealthy implant, an analyst finds it is one module of a larger framework: it resolves APIs by hash, checks for analysis environments, and downloads task modules over encrypted C2. She recognizes a config format and encryption scheme matching a tracked cluster, and reports evidence-bounded findings — shared code and infrastructure link the sample to that cluster — while deferring definitive attribution to the broader intelligence picture.",
refs:[["APT toolkit and modular-framework analysis","Piecing together multi-component implants."],["Responsible attribution methodology","Evidence-bounded linkage and false-flag awareness."]],
kc:[
{q:"APT malware is characteristically:",o:["A single simple file","Modular — a framework of components (implant, task modules, sophisticated C2) analyzed as a system","Always unencrypted","Easy to detect"],a:1,x:"APT toolkits are modular frameworks; analysis must piece together the components, not just one file."},
{q:"Code artifacts useful for linking samples include:",o:["Only the file size","Shared code, reused encryption/config formats, toolmarks, and infrastructure patterns","The user's name","Nothing"],a:1,x:"Reused code, config formats, encryption schemes, and infrastructure link samples to a tracked toolkit or cluster."},
{q:"Responsible attribution means:",o:["Naming a government from one sample","Reporting what evidence supports (shared code/infrastructure) while distinguishing it from unsupported leaps and false flags","Guessing confidently","Ignoring evidence"],a:1,x:"Rigorous attribution bounds claims to the evidence and accounts for reuse and false flags, deferring definitive naming."}]},
{t:"Reporting, Attribution, and Capstone",d:"Turning analysis into intelligence others can act on, and the capstone.",
obj:["Structure a malware analysis report for multiple audiences","Package IOCs and detections for operational use","Complete a full sample analysis extracting config, IPs, and comms"],
read:["Analysis that is not communicated protects no one, so the report is the deliverable, and structuring it for its audiences is a core skill. A complete malware analysis report serves several readers: an executive summary (what the malware is, its impact, and the bottom line, in plain language for decision-makers), a technical analysis (capabilities, behavior, techniques mapped to ATT&CK, and how it works, for responders and engineers), and an IOC/detection appendix (the host and network indicators, YARA and network signatures, and remediation steps, packaged for operational use — modules six, nine, and ten). The report answers the defensive questions that motivated the analysis: how do we detect this, how do we scope the infection, how do we remediate it completely, and what does it tell us about the adversary. Clarity and accuracy matter more than length — a report that responders can act on immediately, with copy-paste-ready IOCs and clear remediation, delivers the analysis's value.",
"The capstone integrates the entire course into the defining task of the discipline: reverse engineer a malicious sample to extract its configuration blocks, target IPs, and communication mechanisms. The analyst applies the full workflow — safe handling and triage (modules one, two) to characterize the sample and spot packing; unpacking and deobfuscation (module eight) to reveal the real code; disassembly and dynamic analysis (modules three through six) to understand behavior; anti-analysis defeat (module eight) if the sample resists; and configuration and communication analysis (modules nine, ten) to locate and decode the config block and C2 routine, extracting the target IPs, the communication protocol and settings, the encryption keys, and any campaign identifier. The deliverable's quality is judged by whether the extracted intelligence is accurate and actionable — correct target IPs and C2 details that can be blocked and detected, a decoded config, and an understanding of the communication mechanism — and whether it is packaged as usable IOCs and detections. This is exactly what defensive malware analysis exists to produce: a determined adversary's sample, safely dissected into the concrete indicators and understanding that let the organization detect, block, remediate, and track the threat. Mastering the report and completing the capstone marks the analyst who can turn hostile code into defensive intelligence."],
ex:"For the capstone, the analyst safely triages a sample, unpacks it under a debugger, defeats an anti-VM check, locates the config referenced before the first C2 call, decodes the RC4 blob to recover three target IPs, the beacon URI and interval, and the RC4 key, and confirms the HTTPS communication mechanism. She packages the IPs and pattern as blocks and a Suricata rule, writes a YARA rule, and delivers a report with an executive summary, technical analysis, and IOC appendix — actionable defensive intelligence from a hostile sample.",
refs:[["Malware analysis report structure and audiences","Executive, technical, and IOC/detection sections."],["End-to-end analysis workflow (triage to intelligence)","Integrating the full toolkit on one sample."]],
kc:[
{q:"A complete malware report is structured for:",o:["Only analysts","Multiple audiences — an executive summary, a technical analysis, and an IOC/detection appendix","Nobody","Only executives"],a:1,x:"The report serves decision-makers, responders/engineers, and operations with tailored sections each can act on."},
{q:"The IOC/detection appendix should be:",o:["Vague prose","Packaged for operational use — host/network indicators, YARA and network signatures, and remediation steps","Omitted","Encrypted"],a:1,x:"Operational, copy-paste-ready IOCs and detections are how the analysis's findings become active defense."},
{q:"The capstone's quality is judged by whether:",o:["The report is long","The extracted intelligence is accurate and actionable — correct target IPs, decoded config, and understood comms, packaged as detections","It uses many tools","It runs fast"],a:1,x:"Accurate, actionable, packaged intelligence (target IPs, config, communication mechanism) is the capstone's measure."}]}
],
cap:[
{q:"SCENARIO: You receive a live malware sample from an active incident. Before anything else, you:",o:["Run it on your workstation","Handle it in an isolated VM with a clean snapshot, no production bridge, and controlled/simulated internet","Email it to colleagues","Double-click it"],a:1,x:"Safe handling — isolation, snapshots, controlled network — is the non-negotiable first step; a live sample is a loaded weapon."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A sample's on-disk file has only three imports and a high-entropy section. This indicates:",o:["It is benign","It is likely packed — resolving its real imports at runtime to hide them from static view","It is corrupt","It has no code"],a:1,x:"Sparse imports and high entropy are classic packing indicators; the real code and imports appear only at runtime."},
{q:"SCENARIO: An automated sandbox reports the sample did nothing malicious. You conclude:",o:["It is safe; close the case","Not proven safe — it may have detected the sandbox and refused to run its real payload; investigate further","Malware is impossible","Delete it"],a:1,x:"Evasive malware behaves benignly under analysis; a clean run is a lead, not a verdict — anti-analysis must be considered."},
{q:"SCENARIO: To unpack a packed sample most reliably, you:",o:["Guess the packer's algorithm by hand","Run it under a debugger, break at the tail jump to the OEP, and dump the unpacked payload from memory","Delete the stub","Give up"],a:1,x:"Letting the malware unpack itself and dumping the payload at the OEP is the reliable general unpacking method."},
{q:"SCENARIO: The sample runs benignly, but you find an early check that exits if a debugger or VM is detected. You:",o:["Accept it is safe","Harden the VM and patch/force the evasion check so the real payload runs and reveals itself","Stop analysis","Reboot"],a:1,x:"Defeating anti-analysis — hardening the environment and neutralizing the checks — forces the concealed payload to run."},
{q:"SCENARIO: You need the sample's C2 addresses and settings. The richest single source is:",o:["The file name","The embedded configuration block — decoded, it yields C2/target IPs, comms settings, keys, and campaign ID","The icon","The file size"],a:1,x:"The config block holds the operational parameters; decoding it is the highest-value extraction for defensive indicators."},
{q:"SCENARIO: The malware's strings and config are encrypted. To recover them you:",o:["Ignore them","Identify and replicate the sample's own decryption routine (e.g., script the XOR/RC4) to decode the strings and config","Delete the sample","Assume they are blank"],a:1,x:"Replicating the malware's decryption algorithm recovers its obfuscated strings and config in bulk."},
{q:"SCENARIO: Content of the C2 traffic is fully encrypted. You can still build a robust detection from:",o:["Nothing","Metadata — beacon timing/pattern, URI structure, user-agent, and TLS/JA3 fingerprint","The ransom note","Luck"],a:1,x:"Traffic metadata yields durable network signatures even when the payload content is encrypted."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A clean-looking process beacons to a C2, but its on-disk file is benign. The likely technique is:",o:["A firewall error","Process injection/hollowing — malicious code runs inside a legitimate process; capture memory to extract it","A false positive to ignore","Nothing"],a:1,x:"In-memory injection makes a legitimate process run malware; memory forensics extracts the injected payload, and detection is behavioral."},
{q:"SCENARIO: Reporting on a sophisticated sample that shares code and infrastructure with a tracked cluster, you:",o:["Publicly name a specific government","Report the evidence-bounded linkage (shared code/infrastructure) and defer definitive attribution, accounting for false flags","Claim certainty","Say nothing"],a:1,x:"Responsible attribution states what the evidence supports and distinguishes it from unsupported leaps, aware of reuse and false flags."}
]};
